-## 2. The Controlled-Evidence Baseline: Real Studies on Ambient Documentation Productivity and Safety
-
-These are the strongest pieces of evidence found across the entire evidence base and anchor the transferability assessments below.
-
-### 2.1 Three-arm pragmatic RCT: DAX Copilot vs Nabla vs control (Lukac et al., NEJM AI)
-
-- **Design:** 238 outpatient physicians, 14 specialties, covariate-constrained 1:1:1 randomization to Microsoft DAX Copilot (n=79), Nabla (n=79), or usual care (n=80); Nov 4, 2024 – Jan 3, 2025. Primary outcome: change in log time-in-note.
-- **Results:** DAX used in 33.5% of 24,696 visits; Nabla in 29.5% of 23,653 visits. **Nabla reduced time-in-note by 9.5% vs control (95% CI −17.2% to −1.8%; P=0.02); DAX showed no significant change (−1.7%; P=0.66).** Secondary survey outcomes improved for both: Mini-Z +2.83 (DAX) / +2.69 (Nabla); physician task load −39.9 / −31.7; work exhaustion −0.32 / −0.23. One grade-1 (mild) adverse event. Clinically significant inaccuracies noted "occasionally" on 5-point Likert items (DAX 2.7, Nabla 2.8); bias "rarely" (1.6 / 1.7). Most common error types: omissions (n=12), structural concerns (n=11), pronoun-resolution errors (n=8).
-- **Critical caveats:** ~15% of treatment physicians never used their assigned scribe; Epic Signal metrics **do not capture time editing drafts inside vendor platforms, so reported time savings are likely overestimates** — a limitation the authors say "affects all AI scribe studies reporting Epic's Signal metrics."
-- **Source type: [PEER-REVIEWED]** — PubMed 41497288 / PMC12768499 (NEJM AI). https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41497288/ (accessed 2026-09-11); full text: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12768499/ (accessed 2026-09-11)
-
-### 2.2 Stepped-wedge RCT of Abridge (UW Health, NEJM AI)
-
-- **Design:** 66 practitioners, 24 weeks (Aug 21, 2024 – Mar 27, 2025), individually randomized stepped-wedge, ITT mixed models, registered on ClinicalTrials.gov (NCT06517082). 71,487 notes authored, 27,092 (38%) ambient-generated; mean utilization 71%.
-- **Results:** Significant reduction in work exhaustion/interpersonal disengagement (−0.44 points on 5-point Likert; 95% CI −0.62 to −0.25; P<0.001; **NNT 1.68**). Professional fulfillment +0.14 (nonsignificant at P=0.04 vs 0.025 threshold). Time spent on notes −0.36 h/day (−21.9 min; robust to sensitivity analysis). Work-outside-work −0.50 h/day but **not robust** — attenuated to −0.19 h (nonsignificant) after removing top 3% of extreme observations. Task load −6.72 (P=0.001).
-- **Billing/safety findings directly relevant to Veritas-Medica:**
-  - **ICD-10 billing compliance improved:** coder-adjudicated compliance score 6.87 (ambient) vs 5.94 (human), P<0.001, on 0–10 scale (n=6,110 sampled notes).
-  - **Note quality (PDSQI-9, LLM-as-judge validated against physician ratings):** accuracy 4.44/5, thoroughness 4.57/5, usefulness 4.83/5, comprehensibility 4.99/5; stigmatizing language <1% of notes (n=12).
-  - **No software drift detected** across 15 rolling 1-month windows via a purpose-built drift-monitoring system.
-  - Patient consent rate 99.92%; zero recording-related complaints.
-- **Caveats:** predominantly younger, white, female, family-medicine cohort of voluntary early adopters; open-label design allows Hawthorne effects.
-- **Source type: [PEER-REVIEWED]** — https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12858090/ (accessed 2026-09-11, NEJM AI; trial registered NCT06517082)
-
-### 2.3 GOSH-led London multi-site evaluation of TORTUS (NHS England sponsored)
-
-- **Design:** 9 NHS sites across London (hospitals, GP practices, mental health, ambulance), 17,000+ patient encounters; led by GOSH DRIVE unit; results announced Sep 4, 2025.
-- **Results:** **+23.5% direct patient interaction time; −8.2% appointment length; A&E: +13.4% patients seen per shift, initial-note time halved; 35% reduction in clinicians feeling overwhelmed by notetaking; 92% patient consent to AI-scribe use.** Economic modelling (York Health Economics Consortium): one extra patient per clinician-shift = £270.93/day added capacity; national A&E scaling would save £176M in documentation time and unlock £658M in capacity annually. Informed NHS England's national AI-scribing guidance and the T.E.S.T. evaluation framework.
-- **Source type: [INDEPENDENT STUDY]** (system-led, NHS-sponsored; economic figures are modelled, not observed) — https://www.gosh.nhs.uk/news/researchgosh-led-trial-of-ai-scribe-technology-shows-transformative-benefits-for-patients-and-clinicians-across-london (published 2025-09-04)
-
-### 2.4 Kent & Medway independent evaluation of TORTUS (the honest negative result)
-
-- **Design:** Independent evaluation by Unity Insights, commissioned by Health Innovation KSS and TORTUS; 7 sites, 4 settings (community paediatrics, acute outpatients, primary care, adult social care); published Dec 1, 2025.
-- **Results:** Primary care saw strongest uptake and perceived time savings ("like my right arm"); most staff considered it safe; over half wished to continue. **But adult social care sites ended their pilot early "after failing to realise the perceived benefits."** Causes were implementation-side: training-to-implementation delays, information-governance delays, software changes, knowledge gaps, communication breakdowns.
-- **Lesson:** ambient-AI value is setting-dependent and implementation-dominated — technology transfer requires per-setting workflow analysis, not blanket rollout.
-- **Source type: [INDEPENDENT STUDY]** — https://healthinnovation-kss.com/independent-evaluation-of-tortus-ambient-voice-technology-shows-time-savings-and-improved-patient-interaction-for-kent-and-medway (published 2025-12-01)
